is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r11
diagnosis_text:
The backbone link on R11 is not down. The OSPF side on R11 (eth1 on segment B, 111.1.1.2) is up and adjacent to R1: “show ip ospf neighbor” on R11 and R1 both show Full state with neighbor 111.1.1.1/111.1.1.2, and R11 successfully pings 10.1.1.1 through the backbone. EIGRP on R11 is also healthy: neighbors are up on eth0 (112.1.1.1 ↔ R12 112.1.1.2) and eth2 (103.1.1.1 ↔ R13 103.1.1.2), and R11 has an EIGRP route to 120.0.0.0/8 via 112.1.1.2.

The symptom (pc12 reaches R12 and R11 but not the backbone) is due to return-path failure in the OSPF domain: R1 lacks any route to 120.0.0.0/8 (“show ip route 120.0.0.0/8” = not in table). Traceroute from pc12 stalls after R12 (120.1.1.1) and R11 (112.1.1.1), consistent with replies being dropped in the backbone for lack of a route to 120/8.

Localization: R11’s OSPF process. The running config shows “router ospf … redistribute connected” but no “redistribute eigrp 100”. As a result, only R11’s connected networks (e.g., 111/8, 112/8, 103/8) are injected into OSPF; EIGRP-learned routes such as 120/8 and 113/24 are not exported.

Root cause: The “redistribute eigrp 100” statement was removed from R11’s OSPF configuration during maintenance.

Corrective fix: On R11, under router ospf, add “redistribute eigrp 100” (optionally with a route-map/metric policy). After committing, verify R1 learns 120.0.0.0/8 and that pc12 can reach backbone prefixes. No physical interface is dead. === END ===